      "revision": "C",
      "title": "Boiler Feed Pump Operating Procedure",
      "doc_type": "sop",
      "section": "\u00a73",
      "heading_path": "3. Normal operating limits",
      "trust_level": "controlled",
      "status": "active",
      "score": 0.03975,
      "similarity": 0.01,
      "matched_alarm_codes": [
        "BFP-SUCT-P-LL"
      ],
      "excerpt": "## 3. Normal operating limits\n\n| Parameter | Normal | Alarm H / L | Alarm HH / LL (trip) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Bearing vibration (velocity, RMS) | < 4.5 mm/s | H: 7.1 mm/s | HH: 11.0 mm/s, pump trips |\n| Bearing temperature | 55\u201375 \u00b0C | H: 85 \u00b0C | HH: 95 \u00b0C, pump trips |\n| Suction pressure | 4.5\u20135.5 barg | L: 3.8 barg | LL: 3.2 barg, pump trips |\n| Discharge pressure | 128\u2013138 barg | L: 120 barg | \u2014 |\n| Flow | 80\u2013220 t/h | Min-flow L: 60 t/h | \u2014 |\n| Mechanical seal leakage | < 10 drops/min | H: leak detection switch | \u2014 |",
      "source_path": "procedures/SOP-BFP-001_boiler_feed_pump_operation.md",
      "cited": false
    },
    {
      "id": "S4",
      "doc_id": "TSG-BFP-002",
      "revision": "B",
      "title": "Boiler Feed Pump Troubleshooting Guide",
      "doc_type": "troubleshooting",
      "section": "\u00a72",
      "heading_path": "2. Recurring high-severity alarms: first questions",
      "trust_level": "controlled",
      "status": "active",
      "score": 0.0395,
      "similarity": 0.0537,
      "matched_alarm_codes": [
        "BFP-SUCT-P-LL"
      ],
      "excerpt": "## 2. Recurring high-severity alarms: first questions\n\nWhen a BFP raises repeated high or critical alarms over weeks or months, answer these before\nreplacing parts:\n\n1. **Do the alarms cluster in time with deaerator alarms?** Repeated DA-101 level or pressure\n   excursions cause repeated low suction pressure and cavitation on the running pump.\n2. **Do they follow load changes?** Rapid boiler load reductions push the pump toward minimum\n   flow. If the recirculation valve responds slowly, vibration alarms follow.\n3. **Do they occur only on one pump?** A pump-specific pattern points to wear, misalignment,\n   or a bearing fault on that pump. A pattern on both pumps points to system causes\n   (deaerator, suction strainer, control tuning).\n4. **Did the pattern start after maintenance?** Post-maintenance misalignment and soft foot\n   are common causes of new vibration alarms.",
      "source_path": "troubleshooting/TSG-BFP-002_boiler_feed_pump_troubleshooting.md",
      "cited": true
    },
    {
      "id": "S5",
      "doc_id": "SAF-002",
      "revision": "B",
      "title": "Emergency Response \u2014 Loss of Boiler Feedwater",
      "doc_type": "safety",
      "section": "\u00a71",
      "heading_path": "1. When this applies",
      "trust_level": "controlled",
      "status": "active",
      "score": 0.03902,
      "similarity": 0.0122,
      "matched_alarm_codes": [
        "BFP-SUCT-P-LL"
      ],
      "excerpt": "## 1. When this applies\n\nUse this procedure when boiler feedwater flow is lost or insufficient, indicated by:\n\n- both boiler feed pumps stopped or tripped, **or**\n- the running pump tripped and the standby pump failed to start, **or**\n- boiler drum level low alarm (`BLR-DRUM-LVL-L`) with feedwater flow below demand.\n\nLow drum level exposes boiler tubes to overheating and can cause tube rupture. This is a\n**priority 1** situation.",
      "source_path": "safety/SAF-002_loss_of_boiler_feedwater_emergency.md",
      "cited": false
    },
    {
      "id": "S6",
      "doc_id": "TSG-BFP-002",
      "revision": "B",
      "title": "Boiler Feed Pump Troubleshooting Guide",
      "doc_type": "troubleshooting",
      "section": "\u00a74",
      "heading_path": "4. Cavitation: the most common cause of recurring BFP alarms",
      "trust_level": "controlled",
      "status": "active",
      "score": 0.03788,
      "similarity": 0.0375,
      "matched_alarm_codes": [
        "BFP-SUCT-P-LL"
      ],
      "excerpt": "## 4. Cavitation: the most common cause of recurring BFP alarms\n\nCavitation happens when suction pressure falls near the vapour pressure of the hot feed water.\nVapour bubbles collapse on the impeller, causing noise, vibration, and erosion.\n\nTypical sequence seen in alarm history: `DA-LVL-L` or `DA-P-L` \u2192 `BFP-SUCT-P-L` \u2192\n`BFP-VIB-H` \u2192 possibly `BFP-VIB-HH` trip.\n\nCorrective actions:\n\n1. Stabilise deaerator level control (check DA-101 level control valve tuning).\n2. Clean the suction strainer if \u0394P exceeds 0.5 bar.\n3. Review deaerator pressure control during load changes.",
      "source_path": "troubleshooting/TSG-BFP-002_boiler_feed_pump_troubleshooting.md",
      "cited": false
    },
    {
      "id": "S7",
      "doc_id": "TSG-BFP-002",
      "revision": "B",
      "title": "Boiler Feed Pump Troubleshooting Guide",
      "doc_type": "troubleshooting",
      "section": "\u00a71",
      "heading_path": "1. How to use this guide",
      "trust_level": "controlled",
      "status": "active",
      "score": 0.03776,
      "similarity": 0.0401,
      "matched_alarm_codes": [
        "BFP-SUCT-P-LL"
      ],
      "excerpt": "## 1. How to use this guide\n\nStart from the alarm code, then work through likely causes in order of probability. Always\ncorrelate with related alarms: a vibration alarm that follows a suction pressure alarm is\nalmost always cavitation, not a mechanical fault.",
      "source_path": "troubleshooting/TSG-BFP-002_boiler_feed_pump_troubleshooting.md",
      "cited": false
    },
    {
      "id": "S8",
